WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.054 --> 00:00:05.179
FAYE MOSS: Welcome to another episode of Superstars

2
00:00:05.180 --> 00:00:12.497
in History ! I'm your host, Faye Moss, here to interview another famous face from

3
00:00:12.498 --> 00:00:18.500
the past. When today's guest was a baby, an illness left her deaf

4
00:00:18.501 --> 00:00:26.498
and blind. With a special teacher, she overcame her disabilities and worked hard to help others overcome

5
00:00:26.499 --> 00:00:48.486
theirs. Please give a warm welcome to Helen

6
00:00:48.487 --> 00:00:52.133
Keller! HELEN KELLER: Hello, Faye. It's lovely to

7
00:00:52.134 --> 00:00:55.736
be here. This is my friend and companion,

8
00:00:55.737 --> 00:01:00.542
Polly Thompson. She will use the manual alphabet to translate your words for

9
00:01:00.543 --> 00:01:03.787
me. FAYE MOSS: I'm delighted that Polly

10
00:01:03.788 --> 00:01:11.289
could join us! Helen, you spent your career communicating in one way or another—writing and giving

11
00:01:11.290 --> 00:01:18.849
lectures and speeches. But as a child, you had few ways to communicate. Do you remember what that

12
00:01:18.850 --> 00:01:25.284
was like? HELEN KELLER: In those early years, I seemed to be in a dense fog, but I wasn't totally cut off

13
00:01:25.285 --> 00:01:30.363
from the world. I could recognize and identify people by the vibrations they made as

14
00:01:30.364 --> 00:01:34.687
they walked. I could also recognize people and objects by

15
00:01:34.688 --> 00:01:41.701
their smell. Even before I learned sign language, I had my own signs for when I was hungry or

16
00:01:41.702 --> 00:01:47.680
sleepy. FAYE MOSS: Eventually your parents called on a very famous

17
00:01:47.681 --> 00:01:54.186
person for advice. HELEN KELLER: Yes! My parents took me to visit Dr. Alexander Graham Bell when I

18
00:01:54.187 --> 00:02:02.444
was six years old. He was well known not only for inventing the telephone but also for finding ways to help

19
00:02:02.445 --> 00:02:08.341
the hearing impaired. As I sat on his lap and felt his long beard, I could

20
00:02:08.342 --> 00:02:14.942
sense his kindness. He recommended that my parents hire a tutor from the Perkins School for

21
00:02:14.943 --> 00:02:20.834
the Blind. FAYE MOSS: And that tutor was Anne Sullivan, who

22
00:02:20.835 --> 00:02:30.208
was herself vision impaired. She came to your home to teach you. You call the day you met Anne in 1887

23
00:02:30.209 --> 00:02:35.388
your soul's birthday. HELEN KELLER: That's right. But our relationship

24
00:02:35.389 --> 00:02:40.058
didn't start off well. When Anne first came to us, I

25
00:02:40.059 --> 00:02:45.811
was a spoiled child. My family let me do whatever I liked because they felt

26
00:02:45.812 --> 00:02:51.030
sorry for me. I often threw tantrums and hit, pinched,

27
00:02:51.031 --> 00:02:55.312
and kicked people. I was easily angered since I could not make people

28
00:02:55.313 --> 00:03:00.707
understand me. Anne used the manual alphabet to spell words into my hand with

29
00:03:00.708 --> 00:03:07.855
her fingers. The first time Anne spelled the word for "cup," I became upset because I didn't understand what she was

30
00:03:07.856 --> 00:03:18.693
doing. I smashed the cup out of frustration! Anne tried to bring some discipline to my life, even though my family couldn't bear it.

31
00:03:18.694 --> 00:03:21.852
FAYE MOSS: But Anne had a plan for that,

32
00:03:21.853 --> 00:03:25.363
didn't she? HELEN KELLER: Anne and I moved into a cottage on

33
00:03:25.364 --> 00:03:31.747
my parents' property. We lived there, just the two of us, so that she could teach me without my

34
00:03:31.748 --> 00:03:39.896
family interfering. At first I just imitated Anne's finger movements without knowing that they

35
00:03:39.897 --> 00:03:43.348
meant anything. Then one day something happened at the

36
00:03:43.349 --> 00:03:48.118
water pump. It seemed as if something long forgotten was coming back to

37
00:03:48.119 --> 00:04:02.331
me. I suddenly knew that the shapes she was finger spelling into my hand—W-A-T-E-R—meant the wonderful, cool substance that was flowing over my other hand. At

38
00:04:02.332 --> 00:04:09.308
that moment the world opened up to me. I had acquired language. FAYE MOSS:

39
00:04:09.309 --> 00:04:11.770
You learned quickly after that.

40
00:04:11.771 --> 00:04:16.566
HELEN KELLER: I exhausted Anne by asking her to spell the names

41
00:04:16.567 --> 00:04:25.536
for things. I wanted to know the names for everything! And I learned to talk, but sadly my speech was hard to

42
00:04:25.537 --> 00:04:33.847
understand. Eventually I attended the Perkins School for the Blind, and after that I attended several other schools for the blind or deaf.

43
00:04:33.848 --> 00:04:40.129
When I was 20 years old, I was accepted to Radcliffe College.

44
00:04:40.130 --> 00:04:48.527
Many people wondered how a deaf-blind young woman would manage at college. But I did well. FAYE

45
00:04:48.528 --> 00:05:00.271
MOSS: I would say so! In 1909 you graduated with honors—and became the first deaf-blind person to earn a Bachelor of Arts degree.

46
00:05:00.272 --> 00:05:07.085
You wrote your first book, The Story of My Life , while you were still a student there.

47
00:05:07.086 --> 00:05:13.003
HELEN KELLER: The studies weren't difficult for me since I loved to learn and read books

48
00:05:13.004 --> 00:05:17.964
in Braille. I particularly enjoyed studying languages like French and

49
00:05:17.965 --> 00:05:26.150
German. But since Radcliffe was not designed for hearing- and vision-impaired students, I depended on Anne to help

50
00:05:26.151 --> 00:05:31.891
me. I was always lucky to have dear companions to finger spell and help me speak.

51
00:05:31.892 --> 00:05:37.536
After Anne died in 1936, Polly stepped in for her. FAYE

52
00:05:37.537 --> 00:05:43.872
MOSS: You were also famous—even as a child. Was this challenging for

53
00:05:43.873 --> 00:05:47.304
you? HELEN KELLER: Being famous could sometimes

54
00:05:47.305 --> 00:05:56.158
be an obstacle. But it also allowed me to meet many powerful and influential people, such as the writer Mark Twain and the actor

55
00:05:56.159 --> 00:06:01.659
Charlie Chaplin. I met every U.S. president from Grover Cleveland to Lyndon

56
00:06:01.660 --> 00:06:09.413
Johnson. I used these connections to advocate for the less fortunate. So I think I put my fame to good use.

57
00:06:09.414 --> 00:06:15.671
FAYE MOSS: Not many people know about this side of you—you were quite

58
00:06:15.672 --> 00:06:18.638
the activist! HELEN KELLER: I was

59
00:06:18.639 --> 00:06:28.113
passionate about many causes. I was one of the founders of the American Civil Liberties Union, an organization that works to protect the Constitutional rights of

60
00:06:28.114 --> 00:06:33.843
the American people. I also worked hard to get women the right to vote, which they won

61
00:06:33.844 --> 00:06:43.313
in 1920. I helped workers fight for the right to organize so they could get better working conditions and better pay. And I was a pacifist

62
00:06:43.314 --> 00:06:49.815
too. I also wrote about issues that were important to me and traveled around the world giving speeches.

63
00:06:49.816 --> 00:06:56.606
I required an interpreter for this, as people sometimes found it difficult to understand my speech.

64
00:06:56.607 --> 00:07:04.341
This was one of my only regrets. I could have helped many more people if I had been able to speak more clearly. FAYE

65
00:07:04.342 --> 00:07:08.981
MOSS: How did you come to care so deeply about helping others?

66
00:07:08.982 --> 00:07:13.781
HELEN KELLER: Early in my career, I worked for the American Foundation for

67
00:07:13.782 --> 00:07:18.828
the Blind. In the position I realized that poor people confront many

68
00:07:18.829 --> 00:07:25.896
obstacles. I wasn't poor, but as a deaf and blind person, I certainly encountered many obstacles

69
00:07:25.897 --> 00:07:33.617
too. So I wanted to help as many people as I could. We all have obstacles of one sort or another, Faye.

70
00:07:33.618 --> 00:07:39.274
FAYE MOSS: That's true, Helen! And now I must thank you for sharing your story with us

71
00:07:39.275 --> 00:07:46.612
today. And thank you, Polly, for assisting with this interview! I'll let you both get back to history now.

72
00:07:46.613 --> 00:07:52.192
HELEN KELLER: Thank you, Faye. It's been

73
00:07:52.193 --> 00:07:57.668
a pleasure. FAYE MOSS: Helen Keller remained an influential figure

74
00:07:57.669 --> 00:08:07.946
even in her eighties. In the early 1960s, an award-winning film brought Helen's story to the big screen, and she received the Presidential

75
00:08:07.947 --> 00:08:12.360
Medal of Freedom. She wrote a dozen books and gave speeches all around

76
00:08:12.361 --> 00:08:18.616
the world. Keller died in 1968, at age 87, in

77
00:08:18.617 --> 00:08:29.015
her home. Her ashes were buried next to her lifelong friends, Anne Sullivan and Polly Thompson, who had died in

78
00:08:29.016 --> 00:08:37.305
1960. Helen Keller's story of overcoming obstacles and advocating for important causes still inspires people today.

79
00:08:37.306 --> 00:08:42.921
I hope you enjoyed today's interview. See you next time!

